Sending keys: ['cat << \'EOF\' > generate_answers.py\nimport json\n\nanswers_data = {\n  "answers": [\n    {\n      "qid": "b2531657ba5c7a78",\n      "status": "ANSWERED",\n      "answer": "PAXLOVID contains ritonavir, which is a strong CYP3A inhibitor. This can lead to increased plasma concentrations of concomitant medications metabolized by CYP3A, potentially resulting in severe, life-threatening, or fatal events. Conversely, concomitant use of strong CYP3A inducers can decrease PAXLOVID concentrations, leading to a loss of therapeutic effect and the potential development of viral resistance. Commonly reported serious adverse reactions due to interactions include calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine) and calcium channel blockers. Prior to prescribing, healthcare providers must review all medications to assess potential interactions and determine if dose adjustments, interruptions, or additional monitoring are required.",\n      "citations": [\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0000",\n          "section": "WARNING: SIGNIFICANT DRUG INTERACTIONS WITH PAXLOVID",\n          "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID includes ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, which may lead to greater exposure of certain concomitant medications, resulting in potentially severe, life-threatening, or fatal events"\n        },\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n          "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n          "supporting_quote": "The most commonly reported concomitant medications resulting in serious adverse reactions were calcineurin inhibitors (e.g., tacrolimus, cyclosporine), followed by calcium channel blockers."\n        }\n      ],\n      "structured_fields": {}\n    },\n    {\n      "qid": "6f12607a1ba14dd5",\n      "status": "ANSWERED",\n      "answer": "Medications that should be avoided (contraindicated) with PAXLOVID include drugs primarily metabolized by CYP3A for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions, and strong CYP3A inducers which significantly reduce PAXLOVID plasma concentrations. Examples of medications to avoid include: alfuzosin, ranolazine, amiodarone, dronedarone, flecainide, propafenone, quinidine, colchicine (in renal/hepatic impairment), lurasidone, pimozide, silodosin, eplerenone, ivabradine, dihydroergotamine, ergotamine, methylergonovine, lovastatin, simvastatin, voclosporin, lomitapide, eletriptan, ubrogepant, finerenone, naloxegol, sildenafil (for PAH), triazolam, oral midazolam, flibanserin, and tolvaptan. Strong CYP3A inducers to avoid include apalutamide, enzalutamide, carbamazepine, phenobarbital, primidone, phenytoin, rifampin, rifapentine, lumacaftor/ivacaftor, and St. John\'s Wort.",\n      "citations": [\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0012",\n          "section": "4 CONTRAINDICATIONS",\n          "supporting_quote": "PAXLOVID is contraindicated with drugs that are primarily metabolized by CYP3A and for which elevated concentrations are associated with serious and/or life-threatening reactions and drugs that are strong CYP3A inducers where significantly reduced nirmatrelvir or ritonavir plasma concentrations may be associated with the potential for loss of virologic response and possible resistance."\n        }\n      ],\n      "structured_fields": {}\n    },\n    {\n      "qid": "fbab4114f66e2dda",\n      "status": "ANSWERED",\n      "answer": "The important warnings and precautions associated with PAXLOVID include: 1) Risk of serious adverse reactions due to drug interactions (strong CYP3A inhibition of concomitant drugs or reduction of PAXLOVID concentrations by inducers); 2) Hypersensitivity reactions, including anaphylaxis, toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN), and Stevens-Johnson syndrome; 3) Hepatotoxicity, including hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice; and 4) Risk of HIV-1 resistance development in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection.",\n      "citations": [\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0013",\n          "section": "5.1 Risk of Serious Adverse Reactions Due to Drug Interactions",\n          "supporting_quote": "Initiation of PAXLOVID, which contains ritonavir, a strong CYP3A inhibitor, in patients receiving medications metabolized by CYP3A... may increase plasma concentrations of medications metabolized by CYP3A."\n        },\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0014",\n          "section": "5.2 Hypersensitivity Reactions",\n          "supporting_quote": "Anaphylaxis, serious skin reactions (including toxic epidermal necrolysis and Stevens-Johnson syndrome), and other hypersensitivity reactions have been reported with PAXLOVID ."\n        },\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0015",\n          "section": "5.3 Hepatotoxicity",\n          "supporting_quote": "Hepatic transaminase elevations, clinical hepatitis, and jaundice have occurred in patients receiving ritonavir."\n        },\n        {\n          "set_id": "8a99d6d6-fd9e-45bb-b1bf-48c7f761232a",\n          "passage_id": "PASSAGE_0016",\n          "section": "5.4 Risk of HIV-1 Resistance Development",\n          "supporting_quote": "Because nirmatrelvir is co-administered with ritonavir, there may be a risk of HIV-1 developing resistance to HIV protease inhibitors in individuals with uncontrolled or undiagnosed HIV-1 infection ."\n        }\n      ],\n      "structured_fields": {}\n    },\n    {\n      "qid": "2d63e0a82215867f",\n      "status": "ANSWERED",\n      "answer": "Safety risks listed for PAXLOVID include: 1